<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="33175" href="schedule/2015/spring/EALC/550/33175.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2015" href="schedule/2015.xml">2015</calendarYear><term id="120151" href="schedule/2015/spring.xml">Spring 2015</term><subject id="EALC" href="schedule/2015/spring/EALC.xml">East Asian Language and Culture</subject><course id="550" href="schedule/2015/spring/EALC/550.xml">Seminar in EALC</course></parents><sectionNumber>G  </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Ming-Qing China</sectionTitle><creditHours>4 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Problems in Early Modern China: Ming-Qing China. This seminar addresses the diverse aspects of historical development in early modern China and will focus specifically on the arguments of newly emerging research. Students will gain an understanding of the complex legacy of the last two imperial dynasties in Chinese history. At the same time, we will examine different historiographical approaches to the study of Ming-Qing China in response to arguments from previous research.</sectionText><sectionNotes>Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign.</sectionNotes><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2015-01-20Z</startDate><endDate>2015-05-06Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>04:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>W      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>1134</roomNumber><buildingName>Foreign Languages Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Kuo" firstName="C">Kuo, C</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>